    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/hawaiiedu/jobs/5450037/program-specialist-food-business-success-coach-0096666t": {
      "title": "Program Specialist (Food Business Success Coach) 0096666T",
      "description": "Career Opportunities at the University of Hawai'i | University of Hawai'i For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. \n        Here are the instructions how to enable JavaScript in your web browser Program Specialist (Food Business Success Coach) 0096666T | University of Hawai'i page has loaded. Program Specialist (Food Business Success Coach) 0096666T | University of Hawai'i page has loaded. Click here or press any key to continue. Career Opportunities at the University of Hawai'i Menu Career Opportunities at the University of Hawai'i Sign In Don't have an account? Create one . Already have an account? Sign In . User profile dropdown menu for Username Applications Inbox Profile Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Applications Inbox Profile Go to Background Investigation Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Welcome! Thanks for creating an account. University of Hawai'i powered by NEOGOV ® Need this page in another language? You can translate this page using your browser's built-in translation feature. Tip: In Chrome or Edge, right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Translate to [Language].\" Program Specialist (Food Business Success Coach) 0096666T Print Apply ﻿ Program Specialist (Food Business Success Coach) 0096666T Salary See Position Description Location UH Maui College, Maui County, HI Job Type Full-Time Temporary Job Number 2026-01808 Department University of Hawai'i Maui College Opening Date 08/14/2026 Closing Date 9/14/2026 11:59 PM Hawaii Position Number: 0096666T Description Title: Program Specialist (Food Business Success Coach) Position Number: 0096666T Hiring Unit: Extended Learning & Workforce Development (Maui Food Innovation Center) Location: Kahului, Maui Date Posted: August 14, 2026 Closing Date: September 14, 2026 Salary: Salary schedules and placement information Additional Salary Information:  Pay Band B: Minimum $5,640/month Full Time/Part Time: Full Time Temporary/Permanent:  Temporary Funding:  Revolving Other Conditions: This is NOT a remote position – the job requires in-person work. POSITION SUMMARY Serves as the Food Business Success Coach for the Maui Food Innovation Center (MFIC) at the University of Hawaii, Maui College (UHMC) under the general supervision of the MFIC Program Manager. Performs complex professional work requiring independent judgment and specialized expertise in small business advising, agribusiness development, value-added food product commercialization, market testing, and cohort coaching. The position delivers 1-on-1 and cohort coaching to local food entrepreneurs and student founders. Key focus areas include translating raw concepts into lean business models, teaching financial literacy and unit economics, guiding customer market validation, and building founder resilience. The position incorporates structured accountability, time optimization, and burnout prevention strategies to support student well-being and academic standing. Additionally, the position plans and executes market entry opportunities (farmers markets, pop-ups) and coordinates workshops and networking events connecting entrepreneurs with wholesalers, retailers, and subject matter experts. Works under general direction, exercising discretion in designing coaching roadmaps, market access strategies, and delivery methods. DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES (** Denotes essential position functions.) ** Direct Business Coaching, Strategic Planning & Skill Building (35%) ** Strategic Planning & Business Modeling: Breaks down raw concepts into realistic, step-by-step business models or lean canvases to help founders systematically translate ideas into executable business plans. ** Financial Literacy & Pricing: Teaches founders how to effectively manage cash flow, set correct pricing structures, and understand basic unit economics to ensure long-term business viability. ** Market Validation & Customer Research: Guides student entrepreneurs through practical customer research and market validation techniques so they avoid spending time and capital on unviable products. ** Mindset & Resilience: Fosters founder resilience and growth mindset by helping entrepreneurs reframe early business failures, setbacks, and pivots into constructive learning lessons. Product-Level Compliance & Food Safety: Advises entrepreneurs on product-level regulatory requirements, labeling standards, and food safety protocols (HACCP/PCQI – hazard analysis critical control point/preventive controls qualified individual) required by state and federal health agencies (Department of Health, Food & Drug Admin., US Dept of Ag) for commercial sale. (Distinct from facility-level compliance managed by leadership). ** Commercialization Coaching: Evaluates product readiness and supply chain needs, connecting clients with local agricultural producers, co-packers, distributors, retail channels, and funding opportunities across Hawai?i. ** Market Entry, Conferences & Networking Event Management (30%) ** Market Entry Events: Plans, coordinates, and implements experiential market entry events, including farmers markets, showcase pop-up events, and retail test-marketing opportunities for client and student products. ** Conferences & Industry Networking: Plans, coordinates, and implements educational conferences, specialized workshops, and structured networking events designed to connect student entrepreneurs with wholesalers, retailers, distributors, buyers, and relevant subject matter experts. ** Logistics & B2B/B2C Preparation: Secures event venues, permits, vendor agreements, and logistics; prepares student and client participants for business-to-business (B2B) and business-to-consumer (B2C) sales settings through pitch coaching, presentation guidelines, and buyer media kits. ** Program Implementation, Accountability & Data Collection (30%) Structured Accountability: Provides a steady, organized cadence of coaching check-ins, milestone tracking, and progress reviews to maintain momentum and combat procrastination among cohort participants. ** Time Optimization & Founder Support: Helps student entrepreneurs balance the day-to-day demands of running a startup by setting realistic operational boundaries to protect mental health and academic standing. ** Cohort Facilitation: Executes and facilitates instructional modules, workshops, and cohort learning activities aligned with MFIC training programs. ** Recruitment & Client Data Collection: Recruits student founders and local food entrepreneurs for coaching programs and events; tracks participant progress, sales metrics, and outcome data (e.g., revenue generated, wholesale contracts secured, jobs created) to support overall MFIC operational reporting led by the Program Manager. Performs other duties as assigned (5%) Minimum Qualifications: Education and Professional Work Experience: Possession of a baccalaureate degree in Agriculture, Agribusiness, Food Science, Culinary Arts, Business Administration, Entrepreneurship, Marketing, Event Management, Education or related field and three to four (3-4) years of progressively responsible professional experience with responsibilities for business coaching/advising, food business incubation/acceleration, event planning/coordination (such as markets, pop-ups, or conferences), or academic program coordination; or any equivalent combination of education and/or professional work experience which provides the required education, knowledge, skills and abilities as indicated. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Considerable working knowledge of principles, practices, and techniques in the area of small business advising, lean canvas business modeling, financial literacy teaching (cash flow, unit economics, pricing), market validation, and agribusiness development as demonstrated by the broad knowledge of the full range of pertinent standard and evolving concepts, principles, and methodologies. Considerable working knowledge and understanding of applicable federal and state laws, rules, regulations, and theories and systems associated with health/safety systems governing food manufacturing and public events (e.g., DOH temporary food permits, FDA, USDA rules). Demonstrated ability to resolve wide-ranging complex problems through the use of creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause of the problems and the resolution of the problems in an effective, innovative, and timely manner. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, verbally, and by preparing reports and other materials. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external organizations, groups, team leaders and members, and individuals. Demonstrated ability to operate a personal computer and apply word processing software (word processing, spreadsheets, presentation tools, event management tools, and database applications). Demonstrated ability to lead subordinates, manage work priorities and projects, and manage employee relations. Desirable Qualifications: Experience utilizing Lean Startup methodologies, Business Model Canvases, or formal business coaching frameworks with early-stage founders. Experience organizing farmers markets, food festivals, trade shows, pop-up retail events, or industry networking conferences. Established professional relationships with local Hawaii retail buyers, wholesalers, food distributors, and agricultural networks. Practical experience starting, owning, operating, or managing a food service, value-added food processing, or retail food business. Background or training in student mentoring, holistic founder support, or mental health awareness/burnout prevention in entrepreneurial settings. Current certification as a ServSafe Manager, Preventive Controls Qualified Individual (PCQI), or HACCP practitioner. The ability to apply knowledge of Native Hawaiian culture to effectively collaborate with multicultural groups and individuals from diverse academic, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds. Inquiries: Name:  UH Maui College, Human Resources Phone:808-984-3381 Email:mauihr@hawaii.edu INCOMPLETE APPLICATIONS WILL NOT BE CONSIDERED To Apply: Click on the \"Apply\" button in the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the required documents. In addition to the Application, applicants must attach the following: A one- to two-page statement outlining ways in which you meet the minimum and desirable qualifications; Official graduate and undergraduate transcript(s) showing degree and coursework to date appropriate to the position ( copies of official transcripts are acceptable ; however, original official transcripts will be required at the time of hire.) Transcripts issued outside the United States of America require a course-by-course analysis with an equivalency statement from an agency having membership with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc., verifying the degree equivalency to that of an accredited institution within the USA. The expense of the evaluation shall be borne by the applicant. Academic record/grade summaries will NOT be accepted. Diplomas and copies will NOT be accepted. Resume Three (3) professional references with contact information (including email addresses) Optional letters of recommendation . Incomplete applications will NOT be considered. NOTE: The application will be incomplete if materials are unreadable, illegible, password-protected, missing, or corrupted; therefore, late or incomplete applications will not be considered.  All requested documents/information become the property of Maui College and will not be returned. Please do not include any self-identifying photos and redact any references to confidential information on submitted documents (social security number, date of birth, etc.). If you have not applied for a position before using NEOGOV, you will need to create an account. EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai'i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status.  For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/ Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai'i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/campus-security/ , or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. In accordance with Article 10 of the unit 08 collective bargaining agreement, bargaining unit members receive priority consideration for APT job vacancies. As a result, external or non BU 08 applicants may not be considered for some APT vacancies. BU 08 members with re-employment rights or priority status are responsible for informing the hiring unit of their status. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai'i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/ Employer University of Hawaii Department University of Hawai'i Maui College Address 310 W. Ka'ahumanu Ave Kahului, Hawaii, 96732 Website http://workatuh.hawaii.edu Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Career Opportunities at the University of Hawai'i Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other accessibility standards. We are continually enhancing our platforms to\n                    ensure greater accessibility, and we actively seek out opportunities to improve accessibility features. Ongoing Enhancements Accessibility is an ongoing effort, and we are regularly updating our technology, services, and content\n         